      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "Despite the seemingly ubiquitous admonition to \"drink at least eight 8-oz glasses of water a day\" (with an accompanying reminder that beverages containing caffeine and alcohol do not count), rigorous proof for this counsel appears to be lacking.",
      "contextBefore": "f physiology. Regulatory, integrative and comparative physiology · 2002-11-01 · 283(5):R993-1004 · DOI: 10.1152/ajpregu.00365.2002 · PMID: 12376390 Publication type: Journal Article, Review Abstract. ",
      "contextAfter": " This review sought to find the origin of this advice (called \"8 x 8\" for short) and to examine the scientific evidence, if any, that might support it. The search included not only electronic modes bu",
      "start": 328,
      "end": 573,
      "extractSha256": "4d0af0069110045105e9b875c53275ac1023640b4a57dca2ee0beff160885159",
      "windowSha256": "a86f16e1b300de945510dbd4108fd65699cd2587d465c2b2e9b6ecd3754f89c0",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T02:56:45.531Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20200307152749/https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12376390",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n4",
      "text": "Become a member to share 10 free articles a month.Become a Plus Member A recent editorial (PDF) in the *Journal of the American Society for Nephrology* is getting wide press coverage for debunking the so-called \"8x8\" theory-the popularly held belief that drinking eight 8-ounce glasses of water daily helps remove toxins, improve skin tone, and increase satiety, among other health benefits.",
      "contextBefore": "ember Comment You have remaining gift available this month. This gift link is valid through . There was an error with your gift link. Please try again. Only Slate Plus members can gift Slate stories. ",
      "contextAfter": " The authors chalk up the belief to folklore, and newspaper reports claim ignorance as to its provenance. Just how long has this idea been around? Two-hundred years, at least. The most commonly cited ",
